window.__finlexDocLoaded(10205,{"id":10205,"articles":[{"id":1000854,"no":"Overview","heading":"Scope & Core Objective (Public Summary)","text":"Governs goodwill impairment testing at the Reporting Unit level (single-step FV < carrying amount), indefinite/finite-lived intangibles, internal-use software (ASC 350-40), and fair value accounting for crypto assets (ASC 350-60, ASU 2023-08).","seq":0},{"id":1000855,"no":"ASC 350-20","heading":"Goodwill Impairment Testing (Reporting Unit Single-Step Model)","text":"Goodwill is NOT amortized (for public business entities) and is tested for impairment at least ANNUALLY (and between annual dates upon a triggering event) at the REPORTING UNIT level (an operating segment or one level below, ASC 350-20-35-34).\n• Optional Qualitative 'Step 0' Screen (35-3A): If more likely than not (>50%) that Reporting Unit fair value exceeds carrying amount, no quantitative test is needed.\n• Quantitative Single-Step Test (ASU 2017-04, 35-4~8B): Compare Reporting Unit FAIR VALUE to carrying amount (including goodwill). Recognize impairment for any excess of carrying amount over fair value, capped at allocated goodwill. Reversal of goodwill impairment is STRICTLY PROHIBITED.\n• Private Company Alternative (35-63): Private companies may elect to amortize goodwill straight-line over <= 10 years and test for impairment only upon a triggering event.","seq":1},{"id":1000856,"no":"ASC 350-40","heading":"Internal-Use Software & Cloud Computing Arrangements (SaaS)","text":"• Three Development Stages (ASC 350-40-25): (1) Preliminary Project Stage → EXPENSE as incurred; (2) Application Development Stage (coding, hardware installation, testing) → CAPITALIZE direct costs & payroll; (3) Post-Implementation / Operation Stage (training, maintenance) → EXPENSE as incurred.\n• Cloud Computing / Hosting Arrangements (ASU 2018-15): Customers in a SaaS hosting arrangement (service contract without a software license) capitalize qualifying implementation costs following ASC 350-40 stages and amortize them over the hosting term into the SAME P&L line as hosting fees.","seq":2},{"id":1000857,"no":"ASC 350-60","heading":"Crypto Assets Fair Value Model (ASU 2023-08) & Cross-Framework Comparison","text":"• Mandatory Fair Value Through Net Income (ASC 350-60, ASU 2023-08, effective FY2025+): Applies to fungible, blockchain-secured crypto assets meeting intangible definition that do NOT provide enforceable rights to underlying goods/services/assets and are not created by the reporting entity (e.g., Bitcoin, Ether; excludes NFTs, wrapped tokens, and stablecoins). Measured at FAIR VALUE each reporting period with changes in NET INCOME, presented separately on the balance sheet and income statement.\n• Key Divergences vs. IAS 36 / IAS 38 / CAS 6 & 8: (1) Goodwill tested at Reporting Unit level via Fair Value under ASC 350 vs. CGU level via Recoverable Amount (higher of FVLCD and VIU) under IAS 36; (2) Indefinite-lived intangible impairment reversal is prohibited under ASC 350-30, permitted under IAS 36; (3) Crypto assets are FVTPL under ASC 350-60 vs. indefinite-lived cost/OCI revaluation under IAS 38 (IFRIC Agenda Decision 2019).","seq":3},{"id":1000858,"no":"Official Access","heading":"Official FASB Codification & Reference Entry","text":"Official Portal (FASB Accounting Standards Codification®): https://asc.fasb.org/\nStatus: in_force (FASB ASC free Basic View available at https://asc.fasb.org/).","seq":4}]});
